// Art Movements Timeline
+ art movements timeline
- Ancient (3000 BCE-500 CE), Medieval (500-1400), Renaissance (1400-1600), Baroque (1600-1750), Neoclassicism (1750-1850), Romanticism (1800-1850), Impressionism (1860-1886), Post-Impressionism (1880-1905), Fauvism (1900-1910), Cubism (1907-1920), Abstract Expressionism (1940s-1960s), Pop Art (1950s-1960s), Contemporary (1970s-present).

+ famous art museums
- The world's great art museums include the Louvre (Paris), Metropolitan Museum (New York), British Museum (London), Uffizi (Florence), Prado (Madrid), Hermitage (St. Petersburg), MoMA (New York), and Guggenheim museums worldwide.

+ art supplies for beginners
- Beginning artists should start with basic supplies: pencils (2H, HB, 2B), erasers, drawing paper, watercolor or acrylic paints, brushes of various sizes, and a sketchbook. Quality doesn't have to be expensive - focus on learning fundamentals first.

+ how to start making art
- Start by drawing what you see around you - objects, people, landscapes. Practice regularly, study the work of artists you admire, take classes or online tutorials, and don't worry about making "good" art at first. Focus on observation and experimentation.

+ difference between art and craft
- The distinction between art and craft is often blurred, but traditionally art emphasizes creative expression and aesthetic experience, while craft focuses on skill and functional objects. However, many contemporary artists work in craft media, and the boundaries continue to evolve.

+ what is public art
- Public art is created for and displayed in public spaces, accessible to all. It includes sculptures, murals, monuments, and installations in parks, streets, and buildings. Public art can beautify spaces, commemorate events, or address social issues.

+ art careers
- Art careers include fine artist, graphic designer, illustrator, art teacher, museum curator, art therapist, art critic, gallery owner, art conservator, and many others. The creative economy offers diverse opportunities combining artistic skills with various specializations.